{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words or Less\n\nI landed in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms were beginning to fall—pink petals drifting like snow over ancient temple roofs. No guidebook could prepare me for the quiet magic of Gion at dawn: wooden machiya houses, the soft clack of geta sandals, and a geisha’s fleeting glance as she vanished down an alley.\n\nI ate ramen at a tiny stall in Fushimi, where the owner didn’t speak English but smiled as he handed me a steaming bowl, topped with a perfect ajitsuke tamago. I followed the path of a thousand red torii gates at Fushimi Inari, each one donated by a business hoping for good fortune. I didn’t pray—I just walked, feeling the weight of centuries in every step.\n\nTokyo was a whirlwind of neon and serenity. In Shibuya, I stood in the famous scramble crossing, surrounded by 1,000 moving souls, yet felt utterly alone in the best way. Later, in the quiet of Meiji Shrine, I pressed my palms together, whispered a wish, and let the rustling cedars carry it away.\n\nI sipped matcha in a tea house where silence was sacred, and bought a single ceramic bowl from a Kyoto artisan who told me, “It’s not about perfect shape. It’s about the hand that holds it.” I didn’t understand then—but I do now.\n\nOn my last night, I took the train to Nara. Deer wandered freely, bowing slightly as I offered them a cracker. One nuzzled my palm, warm and gentle. I thought of how Japan teaches you to find depth in simplicity: a single blossom, a silent bow, a shared meal with a stranger.\n\nI didn’t bring back much—just a few souvenirs, a journal full of scribbles, and a quiet heart. Japan didn’t change me. It reminded me of what I’d forgotten: stillness, respect, and the beauty of things just as they are.\n\nI’m already planning my return.", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0